---
description: Mandatory repository-wide prohibition on secret publication and hardcoded configuration
alwaysApply: true
---

# Secrets and configuration — mandatory for all Cloze backend files

- Applies to code, migrations, seeds, tests, scripts, CI, docs, examples, logs and generated artifacts.
- Never embed or publish real API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, signing secrets, session cookies or credential-bearing connection strings, including encoded or split representations.
- Keep secrets in ignored local environment files or a server-side secret manager. Never expose privileged secrets to clients. Example files contain names and empty values only.
- Never dump environment variables, request headers, config objects, database URLs or Supabase status unfiltered. Tool output, chat and PR descriptions must also be free of secrets. Log an allowlist of safe fields rather than relying only on redaction.
- No hardcoded operational or business configuration: endpoints, ports, project/user IDs, machine paths, model identifiers, quotas, prices, timeouts, retry counts and feature flags must come from validated environment configuration or managed settings.
- Moving a literal into a named constant is not externalizing configuration. No `Deno.env.get("SETTING") || "value"`, `?? "value"`, demo credentials or silent production defaults.
- Centralize configuration parsing and type/range/format validation. A missing or invalid required value stops the operation before side effects; diagnostics include only the setting name.
- Non-secret local tooling values may live in dedicated versioned configuration such as `supabase/config.toml`; never copy them into application logic. Production values must be supplied explicitly for that environment.
- Protocol constants (HTTP status codes), schema identifiers and pinned dependency versions belong to their contracts/manifests. Synthetic test inputs must be clearly artificial and isolated in test fixtures; tests inject configuration and never use real credentials.
- For example: `const timeoutMs = config.requestTimeoutMs` is acceptable after validation; `const timeoutMs = 5000` in a handler is not.
- Before commit, push, PR publication or deployment, inspect the exact outgoing files/artifacts and run a secret scanner with redacted output. Scan outgoing Git history too when publishing commits. Review hardcoded configuration separately; a secret scanner does not detect all such values.
- If scanning cannot run or a finding remains unresolved, block publication and explain the missing verification. Never disable checks, use bypass flags or add broad allowlists to make a scan pass.
- Any false-positive exception must be narrow, justified and reviewable; real secrets never qualify. If exposure occurred, require revocation/rotation and history/artifact remediation, not only removal from the current file.
